We start by expressing $x$ and $y$ in terms of a parameter $t$ as follows:
$$
\begin{align*}
x &= \cos(t)\\
y &= \sin(t)
\end{align*}
$$
We then have:
$$
\begin{align*}
| |x| + |y| | &= | \cos(t) + \sin(t) |\\
&= \sqrt{2 + 2 \cos(t)}\\
&= \sqrt{2 (1 + \cos(t))}
\end{align*}
$$
To maximize this expression, we need to maximize $1 + \cos(t)$, which occurs when $t = 0$. Therefore, the maximum value of $| |x| + |y| |$ is $\boxed{\sqrt{2}}$.
